---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS): Bayesian Shrinkage & The Portability Crisis";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical derivation of Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS): Clumping & Thresholding (C+T), LDpred2 spike-and-slab MCMC, PRS-CS continuous shrinkage hierarchy, clinical calibration metrics, and the trans-ethnic portability crisis.";
/* Derived from this page's own content: prose at 200 wpm, inline math priced by
   source length, and 15 s per display equation — the same model `lessonReadingTime`
   applies to the migrated lessons, so both renderers agree. Part 2 of the migration
   deletes this constant along with the page. */
const readingTime = "7 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 8</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Methodological</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      Can we predict an individual's lifetime risk of developing coronary artery disease, breast cancer, or type 2 diabetes solely from their inherited DNA genotype? <strong>Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS)</strong> synthesize summary statistics across millions of common variants into a single individual risk metric. This deep dive explores the mathematical foundations of PRS: <strong>Clumping and Thresholding (C+T)</strong>, <strong>LDpred2 Bayesian spike-and-slab Gibbs sampling</strong>, the <strong>PRS-CS linked continuous shrinkage hierarchy</strong>, <strong>clinical calibration metrics (Brier score, AUC, OR/SD)</strong>, and the scientific drivers behind the <strong>trans-ethnic portability crisis</strong>.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#formulation">1. The Additive Polygenic Risk Score Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ct">2. Pruning & Thresholding (C+T / P+T)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ldpred2">3. Joint LD Modeling & LDpred2 (Privé et al. 2020)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#prscs">4. PRS-CS: The Linked Continuous Shrinkage Hierarchy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#evaluation">5. Statistical & Clinical Evaluation Metrics</a></li>
        <li><a href="#portability">6. The Cross-Population Portability Crisis & PRS-CSx</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="formulation">
      <h2>1. The Additive Polygenic Risk Score Formulation</h2>
      <p>
        For individual <Katex tex="i" /> with standardized genotype dosages <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> across <Katex tex="M" /> common variants, the linear <strong>Polygenic Risk Score (<Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i" />)</strong> is:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Fundamental Polygenic Risk Score Equation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i = \sum_{j=1}^{M} w_j G_{ij}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="w_j" /> = Posterior / conditional effect size assigned to variant <Katex tex="j" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="G_{ij}" /> = Allele dosage (number of risk alleles carried by individual <Katex tex="i" />).
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The central mathematical challenge in PRS construction is estimating conditional weights <Katex tex="w_j" /> that adjust for Linkage Disequilibrium correlation between SNPs without overfitting the discovery GWAS summary statistics.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="ct">
      <h2>2. Pruning & Thresholding (C+T / P+T)</h2>
      <p>
        The classical heuristic approach (Purcell et al. 2009) filters SNPs via two sequential steps:
        <br/>
        1. <strong>Clumping (LD Pruning):</strong> Iteratively removes SNPs that are in strong Linkage Disequilibrium (<Katex tex="r^2 > 0.1" /> within a 250 kb window) with a more significantly associated index SNP.
        <br/>
        2. <strong>Thresholding:</strong> Retains clumped SNPs satisfying a P-value cutoff <Katex tex="P_j < P_T" />, setting <Katex tex="w_j = \hat{\beta}_j" /> for retained variants and <Katex tex="w_j = 0" /> otherwise:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">C+T Indicator Weighting</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="w_j = \hat{\beta}_j \cdot \mathbb{I}(P_j < P_T) \cdot \mathbb{I}(j \in \text{Clumped SNPs})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          The optimal threshold <Katex tex="P_T \in \{5 \times 10^{-8}, 10^{-6}, 10^{-4}, 0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.5, 1.0\}" /> is selected via cross-validation in an independent tuning dataset.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="ldpred2">
      <h2>3. Joint LD Modeling & LDpred2 (Privé et al. 2020)</h2>
      <p>
        C+T discards millions of SNPs, losing valuable polygenic signal. <strong>LDpred2 (Privé et al. 2020 Bioinformatics)</strong> models the full LD correlation matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{R}" /> under a Bayesian <strong>spike-and-slab prior</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">LDpred2 Spike-and-Slab Prior</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\beta_j \sim \pi \mathcal{N}\left(0, \frac{h^2}{M \pi}\right) + (1 - \pi) \delta_0" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\pi" /> = Polygenicity parameter (proportion of causal variants) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\delta_0" /> = Dirac delta spike at zero &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="h^2" /> = SNP heritability estimated via LDSC.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        LDpred2 samples posterior effect sizes <Katex tex="\mathbf{w}" /> using Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) Gibbs sampling:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\beta_j \mid \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}, \mathbf{R}] \approx \frac{\tilde{z}_j}{\sqrt{N}} \cdot \frac{N / (N + M \pi / h^2)}{1 + \frac{1 - \pi}{\pi} \sqrt{1 + \frac{N h^2}{M \pi}} \exp\left( -\frac{\tilde{z}_j^2}{2} \frac{N h^2 / (M\pi)}{1 + N h^2 / (M\pi)} \right)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\tilde{z}_j = \sqrt{N}\hat{\beta}_j - \sum_{k \ne j} r_{jk} \sqrt{N} \beta_k^{(t)}" /> is the residual Z-score conditioned on the current state of all other SNPs.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="prscs">
      <h2>4. PRS-CS: The Linked Continuous Shrinkage Hierarchy</h2>
      <p>
        MCMC sampling under spike-and-slab priors can struggle with multimodal posterior spaces. <strong>PRS-CS (Ge, Chen, Ni, Feng, and Smoller 2019 Nature Communications)</strong> places a continuous shrinkage prior on effect sizes:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">PRS-CS Linked Hierarchical Prior (P0 Exact Formulation)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\beta_j \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \frac{\sigma^2}{N} \phi \psi_j\right), \qquad \psi_j \sim \mathcal{G}(a, \delta_j), \qquad \delta_j \sim \mathcal{G}(b, 1)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\phi" /> = Global shrinkage parameter (scaling overall polygenicity across the genome) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\psi_j" /> = Local shrinkage parameter (allowing large-effect variants to escape shrinkage) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\mathcal{G}(\alpha, \beta)" /> = Gamma distribution with shape <Katex tex="\alpha" /> and rate <Katex tex="\beta" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="a = 1, b = 0.5" /> (Strawderman-Berger prior).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> PRS-CS-auto: Fully Bayesian Hyperparameter Learning
        </div>
        <p>
          While standard PRS-CS sweeps fixed global parameters <Katex tex="\phi \in \{10^{-6}, 10^{-4}, 10^{-2}, 1\}" />, <strong>PRS-CS-auto</strong> places a Half-Cauchy hyperprior on the global parameter:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\sqrt{\phi} \sim \mathcal{C}^+(0, 1)" display={true} />
          PRS-CS-auto learns <Katex tex="\phi" /> directly from the GWAS summary statistics and LD reference panel without requiring a separate tuning validation cohort!
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="evaluation">
      <h2>5. Statistical & Clinical Evaluation Metrics</h2>

      <h3>1. Incremental Variance Explained (ΔR²)</h3>
      <p>
        Measures the additional phenotypic variance explained by the PRS above baseline demographic covariates (age, sex, top 10 ancestry PCs):
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\Delta R^2 = R^2_{\text{Full Model}} - R^2_{\text{Covariates Only}}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>2. Odds Ratio per Standard Deviation (OR / SD)</h3>
      <p>
        When evaluating standardized PRS (<Katex tex="\mu = 0, \sigma = 1" />) in logistic regression:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{OR}_{\text{per SD}} = \exp(\beta_{\text{PRS}})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          For Coronary Artery Disease, individuals in the top 5% of the PRS distribution experience a <strong>3- to 4-fold elevated lifetime risk</strong> (<Katex tex="\text{OR} \approx 3.5" />) compared to the population median (Khera et al. 2018).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>3. Brier Score & Calibration</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Brier Score Decomposition</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{BS} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (y_i - \hat{p}_i)^2 = \text{Reliability} - \text{Resolution} + \text{Uncertainty}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="portability">
      <h2>6. The Cross-Population Portability Crisis & PRS-CSx</h2>
      <p>
        Over 75% of existing GWAS participants are of European genetic ancestry. A landmark study by <strong>Martin et al. (2019 Nature Genetics)</strong> revealed a severe <strong>portability crisis</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Target Population Ancestry</th>
              <th>Relative Prediction Accuracy (vs. European)</th>
              <th>Primary Driver of Attenuation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>European (EUR)</strong></td>
              <td><strong>100% (Baseline)</strong></td>
              <td>Matched discovery cohort LD architecture.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>East Asian (EAS)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\approx 45\text{--}55\%" /></td>
              <td>Moderate LD decay divergence and MAF shifts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>South Asian (SAS)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\approx 50\text{--}60\%" /></td>
              <td>Haplotype frequency differences.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Hispanic / Latino (AMR)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\approx 50\text{--}65\%" /></td>
              <td>Complex ancestral admixture blocks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>African (AFR)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\approx 20\text{--}25\%" /></td>
              <td><strong>Much shorter LD blocks</strong> and immense genetic diversity breaking tag SNP correlations.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Multi-Ancestry Solution: PRS-CSx (Ruan et al. 2022)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>PRS-CSx</strong> integrates GWAS summary statistics from multiple diverse populations simultaneously, utilizing ancestry-matched LD reference panels while coupling posterior effect sizes via a shared continuous shrinkage prior:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">PRS-CSx Multi-Ancestry Prediction Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \alpha_k \left(\sum_{j=1}^M w_{jk} G_{ij}\right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="w_{jk}" /> = Posterior effect sizes derived using ancestry-specific LD panels <Katex tex="\mathbf{R}_k" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\alpha_k" /> = Meta-weights optimized via cross-validation in the target cohort.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Additive PRS:</strong> <Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i = \sum w_j G_{ij}" /> aggregates genome-wide polygenic susceptibility.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>LDpred2:</strong> Uses spike-and-slab Gibbs sampling to model LD correlation matrices.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>PRS-CS:</strong> Places continuous Strawderman-Berger shrinkage on effect sizes; PRS-CS-auto learns <Katex tex="\phi" /> via Half-Cauchy hyperpriors.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Portability Crisis:</strong> European-trained scores lose up to 75% accuracy in African ancestry cohorts, addressed by multi-ancestry PRS-CSx.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: Causal Inference & Mendelian Randomization (MR)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 9 to explore instrumental variable methods (IVW, MR-Egger, Weighted Median) that leverage genetic assortment to infer causal relationships in observational epidemiology.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-mendelian-randomization/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 9: Mendelian Randomization</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
